    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d601">

                <pb facs="1225" n="1203" sameAs="frus:frus1946v01/pg1203"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.2423/1–546</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Secretary of
                            State to President Truman
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-01-05">January 5, 1946</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The British have requested that a British scientific team be permitted to attend
                    the forthcoming atomic bomb trials.<anchor n="4" xml:id="d601-f1"/> The
                    Secretaries of State, War, and Navy approve the idea, particularly since the
                    British have various experts with special knowledge who would be of considerable
                    assistance in connection with the tests, and from the military standpoint their
                    participation would be desirable. The officer in charge of the atomic bomb
                        project<anchor n="5" xml:id="d601-f2"/> has indicated informally that he has
                    no objection.</p>
                <p>We should like to have your approval before proceeding.<anchor n="6"
                        xml:id="d601-f3"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">James</hi> F. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Byrnes</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d601-f1" n="4" xml:id="d601-f1-content">
                <p>For information regarding the origins of the Bikini tests, see Hewlett and
                    Anderson, pp. 581–582.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d601-f2" n="5" xml:id="d601-f2-content">
                <p>Vice Adm. William H. P. Blandy.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d601-f3" n="6" xml:id="d601-f3-content">
                <p>The President’s initialled “OK” appears on the source text.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
